  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1130 PM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nFriday night through Wednesday:\n\nSynopsis: Low pressure will slowly continue east into the Upper \nMississippi Valley Friday night, with deepening warm advection & low \nlevel jet dynamics supporting widespread areas of shower and \nthunderstorm development. An unseasonably moist environment will \nsupport heavy downpours in any thunderstorms, with locally heavy \nrainfall possible. The low will continue east toward southern \nWisconsin during the day Saturday, slowly pulling a trailing cold \nfront through the region during the afternoon and evening hours. \nAdditional shower/storms & heavy downpour potential may accompany \nthe passing front, though locations & coverage of development will \ndepend on frontal positioning and & thermodynamic recoveries \noccurring in the wake of earlier day precip. Severe weather \npotential currently appears low in any storms occurring Friday night \nthrough Saturday evening, though trends will continue to be \nmonitored for isolated stronger storms with gusty winds in coming \nupdates. High pressure will build into Ontario Saturday night into \nSunday, shoving the cold front south of the area. An upper \ndisturbance will move in from the Northern Plains on Monday, \nbringing additional chances for non-impactful rain showers. \nConditions will dry out by mid-week. \n\nFriday night through Saturday morning: Widespread showers and storms \nwill overspread the area as a strengthening low level jet allows \nwarm/moist advection to blossom regionally. Apart from maintaining \nshower & storm development, the warm/moist advection will transport \nunseasonably moist air with precipitable water values on the order \nof 1.7\"+ into southern Wisconsin, supporting enhanced rainfall rates \nin any thunderstorms. Presence of this air mass & anticipated \nwidespread storm coverage thus makes this part of the period one to \nwatch for locally heavy rainfall and possible hydrologic impacts \n(urban/small stream flooding, rises on rivers/streams & isolated \nflash flooding). Modest but still well-defined deep layer wind \nfields point toward progressive storm motions, with the \nmaterialization of hydro impacts likely requiring sustained periods \nof training. Will thus be watching for any definitive training \nsignals as CAMs continue to come into range over the next 12-24 \nhours. Overnight storm timing should limit organized severe weather \npotential.\n\nSaturday afternoon & evening: Depending on a combination 1) \npositioning of the passing cold front and 2) the relative amounts of \nclouds versus sunshine materializing in the wake of \novernight/morning storms, an additional batch of shower and storm \ndevelopment is possible across parts of the area. The aforementioned \nuncertainties in mesoscale environment make it difficult to \nconfidently pinpoint where additional storm development may be \nfavored, with trends being monitored into this weekend. \nEnvironmental uncertainties aside, still unseasonably high moisture \nwould support additional heavy downpour potential in any \nthunderstorms.\n\nSunday through Tuesday: While the NBM is populating with precip \nprobabilities through a large portion of this time frame, suspect \nthat the best window for additional rain will be during the day on \nMonday when a disturbance is forecast to move into the western Great \nLakes. Will be monitoring for possible refinements as this portion \nof the period draws closer. Not expecting hydro impacts given much \nlower precipitable water values in the wake of the weekend front. \nExpect a drying trend from Tuesday onward with Canadian high \npressure progged to build into the western Great Lakes.\n\nQuigley",
